            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Classification of Minerals: Nature, Categories & Industrial Utility</h3>\n            <p>A <strong>mineral</strong> is a naturally occurring, homogeneous, inorganic substance possessing a definite chemical composition and an ordered internal atomic structure. In physical and economic geography, India’s mineral resources are systematically classified based on their chemical composition, metallic content, and industrial applications.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Primary Classification of Mineral Resources</h4>\n            <p>India’s minerals are broadly categorized into four primary groups:</p>\n            <ol>\n                <li><strong>Metallic Minerals:</strong> Minerals that yield malleable, ductile metals with metallic luster upon smelting. They are further divided into:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Ferrous Minerals:</strong> Contain iron content and are fundamental to metallurgical and heavy engineering industries. Examples: <em>Iron ore, Manganese, Chromite, Nickel, Cobalt, and Pyrite</em>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Non-Ferrous Minerals:</strong> Devoid of iron content, highly resistant to corrosion, ductile, and vital for electrical, electronics, aviation, and automobile sectors. Examples: <em>Bauxite (aluminium), Copper, Lead, Zinc, Tin, and Antimony</em>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Precious Metals:</strong> High economic value, rare occurrence, non-reactive. Examples: <em>Gold, Silver, Platinum</em>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Non-Metallic Minerals:</strong> Inorganic minerals that do not yield metals upon processing, often characterized by brittle texture and high electrical and thermal resistance. Examples: <em>Mica, Limestone, Dolomite, Gypsum, Asbestos, Magnesite, Silica sand, Feldspar, and Kaolin (China clay)</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Energy / Mineral Fuels:</strong> Organic or chemical substances extracted from the earth’s crust that release significant thermal and electrical energy through combustion. Examples: <em>Coal, Lignite, Petroleum (Crude oil), and Natural Gas</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Atomic / Nuclear Minerals:</strong> Heavy radioactive minerals capable of nuclear fission to generate nuclear power and defense materials. Examples: <em>Uranium (pitchblende), Thorium (monazite sands), Zircon, Beryl, and Lithium</em>.</li>\n            </ol>\n\n            <h4>2. Physical Properties Governing Extraction</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Tenor of Ore:</strong> The percentage of pure metal present in the mined mineral ore. Higher tenor reduces smelting costs and environmental slag.</li>\n                <li><strong>Occurrence Forms:</strong> Minerals occur in igneous and metamorphic veins and lodes (e.g., copper, zinc, lead), sedimentary beds and layers (e.g., coal, iron ore, limestone), residual weathering mantles (e.g., bauxite), and alluvial/placer beach sands (e.g., gold, monazite).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
