            "title": "Geological Foundations of Indian Minerals: Dharwar, Gondwana, Cuddapah & Vindhyan Systems",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Geological Foundations of Indian Minerals: The Stratigraphic Matrix</h3>\n            <p>India’s rich and diverse mineral endowment is a direct outcome of its complex geological evolutionary history spanning over 3.5 billion years. The geological formations of the Peninsular Shield house over 95% of India’s metallic, coal, and non-metallic mineral wealth.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Archean and Dharwar Rock Systems (Metallic Storehouse)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Age and Genesis:</strong> Formed during the Archean and Early Proterozoic eons (2.5 to 3.8 billion years ago). The <strong>Dharwar System</strong> represents the earliest metamorphosed sedimentary and volcanic rock complex in India.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mineral Endowment:</strong> The Dharwar System is <strong>India’s premier mineral treasure house</strong>. It contains virtually all primary metallic minerals:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Iron Ore:</strong> Giant hematite and magnetite formations in Karnataka (Kudremukh, Bababudan Hills, Sandur), Odisha, and Jharkhand.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Manganese:</strong> Found in gonditic and koduritic rock suites.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Gold:</strong> The historic quartz lode veins of Kolar Gold Fields (KGF) and Hatti in Karnataka.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Copper & Chromite:</strong> Singhbhum copper belt (Jharkhand) and Sukinda chromite (Odisha).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Gondwana Rock System (Coal Repository)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Age and Genesis:</strong> Deposited during the Upper Carboniferous to Jurassic periods (250–150 million years ago) in extensive riverine rift valleys and tectonic grabens.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mineral Wealth:</strong> The Gondwana rock system contains over <strong>98% of India’s total coal reserves</strong> and 99% of its domestic coal production.</li>\n                <li><strong>Major Basins:</strong> Located along down-faulted river troughs: Damodar Valley (Jharkhand/West Bengal), Son Valley (Madhya Pradesh/UP), Mahanadi Valley (Odisha/Chhattisgarh), and Wardha-Godavari Valley (Maharashtra/Telangana).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Cuddapah and Vindhyan Rock Systems (Building Materials & Industrial Minerals)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Cuddapah System:</strong> Unfossiliferous sedimentary-metamorphic rocks deposited in crescent-shaped synclinal basins (e.g., Cuddapah basin of Andhra Pradesh). Rich in high-grade <em>limestone, dolomite, asbestos, barytes, steatite, and iron ore</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Vindhyan System:</strong> Spanning from Sasaram in Bihar to Chittorgarh in Rajasthan. Celebrated for monumental dimension stones (<em>red sandstone</em> used in Red Fort, Jama Masjid, and Fatehpur Sikri), ornamental marbles, cement-grade limestone, and <strong>diamondiferous kimberlite pipes</strong> at Panna (Madhya Pradesh) and Majhgawan.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Tertiary Rock Formations (Hydrocarbons & Young Coal)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Formed during the Cenozoic era (65 to 2 million years ago). Associated with marine and estuarine sedimentation in Assam, Gujarat, and offshore continental shelves, forming traps for <strong>crude petroleum and natural gas</strong>.</li>\n                <li>Contains young, high-moisture <strong>Lignite coal</strong> (e.g., Neyveli in Tamil Nadu, Palana in Rajasthan) and Tertiary coal in Meghalaya and Assam.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
