            "title": "Major Mineral Belts of India: The Five Geographic Provinces",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Major Mineral Belts of India: The Five Geographic Provinces</h3>\n            <p>Minerals are not distributed uniformly across the Indian landmass; rather, they are concentrated in three to five well-defined regional geological clusters or <strong>mineral belts</strong>, primarily situated across the ancient peninsular plateau.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The North-Eastern Peninsular Belt (The Ruhr of India)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographic Extent:</strong> Encompasses the Chota Nagpur Plateau (Jharkhand), West Bengal, and northern Odisha.</li>\n                <li><strong>Economic Significance:</strong> Known as the <em>'Ruhr of India'</em> (after Germany's industrial coal and steel heartland). This is India’s richest and most heavily industrialized mineral province.</li>\n                <li><strong>Key Minerals:</strong> Vast, high-grade deposits of <strong>iron ore</strong> (Noamundi, Badampahar, Gurumahisani), <strong>coking coal</strong> (Jharia, Raniganj, Bokaro), <strong>bauxite</strong> (Lohardaga), <strong>copper</strong> (Singhbhum), <strong>chromite</strong> (Sukinda), <strong>manganese</strong> (Keonjhar), and <strong>uranium</strong> (Jadugoda).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Central Belt</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographic Extent:</strong> Covers Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh, eastern Maharashtra, and parts of southern Uttar Pradesh.</li>\n                <li><strong>Key Minerals:</strong> Holds the second largest mineral concentrations in the country:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Iron Ore:</strong> World-class mechanized mines at <strong>Bailadila</strong> (Dantewada, Chhattisgarh) and Dalli-Rajhara.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Manganese:</strong> High-grade deposits in Balaghat and Chhindwara (MP) and Nagpur-Bhandara (Maharashtra).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Bauxite:</strong> Amarkantak and Maikala plateaus.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Coal:</strong> Korba, Singrauli, Pench-Kanhan, and Wardha valley.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Copper:</strong> Malajkhand (Balaghat, MP - largest open-pit copper mine in India).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Limestone:</strong> Expansive cement-grade limestone belts in Chhattisgarh and MP.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Southern Belt</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographic Extent:</strong> Karnataka Plateau (Bellary, Hospet, Chitradurga, Chikmagalur) and adjoining high Tamil Nadu uplands.</li>\n                <li><strong>Key Minerals:</strong> Renowned for high-grade <strong>iron ore</strong> (Bababudan Hills, Kudremukh, Sandur), <strong>manganese</strong>, <strong>chromite</strong>, <strong>gold</strong> (Hatti gold mines in Raichur), and vast <strong>lignite</strong> deposits at Neyveli in Tamil Nadu. Lacks significant coking coal deposits.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. The South-Western Belt</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographic Extent:</strong> Covers southern Maharashtra (Ratnagiri, Sindhudurg) and the state of Goa.</li>\n                <li><strong>Key Minerals:</strong> Characterized by proximity to maritime ports for direct export. Major deposits of <strong>iron ore</strong> (Bicholim, Kudnem in Goa, exported via Mormugao port), <strong>manganese</strong>, and residual <strong>bauxite</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>5. The North-Western Belt</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Geographic Extent:</strong> Extends along the Aravalli Range in Rajasthan and into adjoining Gujarat.</li>\n                <li><strong>Key Minerals:</strong> The non-ferrous and industrial stone hub of India:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Lead and Zinc:</strong> Zawar, Rampura-Agucha, Rajpura-Dariba (Rajasthan holds over 85% of Indian resources).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Copper:</strong> Khetri-Singhana belt (Jhunjhunu, Rajasthan).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Petroleum:</strong> Barmer basin (Mangala, Bhagyam oilfields in Rajasthan) and Cambay basin/Ankleshwar (Gujarat).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Building Stones & Non-Metallics:</strong> Makrana marble, Kota stone, gypsum, silica sand, and rock phosphate (Jhamarkotra).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>6. The Himalayan and Coastal Belts</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Himalayas:</strong> Geologically folded and complex, containing polymetallic sulfide ores (copper, lead, zinc, cobalt, nickel) in Sikkim, J&K, and Himachal, but extraction is limited by topography and ecological sensitivity.</li>\n                <li><strong>Coastal Placer Sands:</strong> Heavy mineral sands along Kerala, Tamil Nadu, and Odisha containing <em>ilmenite, rutile, zircon, and thorium-rich monazite</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
