            "id": "m05",
            "title": "The MMDR Act (1957) & Milestone Amendments: 2015, 2021 & The Critical Minerals Reform (2023)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>The MMDR Act (1957) & Milestone Legislative Amendments</h3>\n            <p>The <strong>Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957 (MMDR Act)</strong> is the foundational legislation governing the development, regulation, lease allocation, and conservation of minerals across India. Between 2015 and 2023, the Act underwent structural amendments that revolutionized the mining economy.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The MMDR Amendment Act, 2015: The Transparency Revolution</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Mandatory Auction Regime:</strong> Replaced the opaque, discretionary 'First-Come-First-Served' system and discretionary renewal of mining leases with <strong>mandatory, transparent electronic competitive auctions (e-auctions)</strong> for both Mining Leases (ML) and Composite Licenses (Prospecting License-cum-Mining Lease).</li>\n                <li><strong>Fixed 50-Year Tenure:</strong> Extended the tenure of all mining leases granted through auction to a uniform <strong>period of 50 years</strong>, eliminating arbitrary periodic renewal negotiations.</li>\n                <li><strong>Institutional Additions:</strong>\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>District Mineral Foundation (DMF):</strong> Established as a non-profit trust in all mining-affected districts, funded by mining leaseholders (10% of royalty for auction leases, 30% for pre-2015 leases) to benefit local and tribal communities.</li>\n                        <li><strong>National Mineral Exploration Trust (NMET):</strong> Created to accelerate deep-seated regional mineral exploration, funded by an extra 2% levy on royalties paid by leaseholders.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The MMDR Amendment Act, 2021: Maximizing Production</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Removal of Captive vs Merchant Distinction:</strong> Abolished the artificial barrier between captive mines (tied exclusively to a specific steel/cement plant) and merchant mines. Allowed captive mines to sell up to 50% of their annual mineral production in the open market after meeting their own plant requirements.</li>\n                <li><strong>Transfer of Non-Auctioned Leases:</strong> Permitted the transfer of statutory clearances and mining leases without recurring state approval charges.</li>\n                <li><strong>Vesting of Powers in Centre:</strong> Empowered the Central Government to conduct auctions of mineral blocks if State Governments fail to conduct auctions within prescribed statutory time limits.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The MMDR Amendment Act, 2023: The Critical Minerals Leap</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Introduction of Exploration Licence (EL):</strong> Created a novel mineral concession—the <em>Exploration Licence</em>—for 29 critical and deep-seated minerals (e.g., lithium, cobalt, nickel, platinum, copper, rare earth elements). Authorized junior private exploration companies to conduct advanced reconnaissance and prospecting.</li>\n                <li><strong>Delisting of Six Atomic Minerals:</strong> Removed <strong>six atomic minerals</strong>—including <em>Lithium, Beryllium, Titanium, Niobium, Tantalum, and Zirconium</em>—from the restricted list of atomic minerals under Schedule 1. This opened up these minerals for commercial private exploration, mining, and auction.</li>\n                <li><strong>Empowering Centre to Auction Critical Minerals:</strong> Empowered the Central Government to exclusively conduct competitive auctions for <strong>24 designated Critical and Strategic Minerals</strong> (including Lithium, Graphite, Cobalt, REEs, Potash, and Nickel), even though the resulting royalty revenues continue to accrue directly to the respective State Governments.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The MMDR Act 1957 is the principal legislation governing India's mineral extraction and leasing.",
                "The 2015 Amendment made competitive e-auctions mandatory for granting mining leases and fixed lease tenure at 50 years.",
                "The 2015 Amendment established the District Mineral Foundation (DMF) and National Mineral Exploration Trust (NMET).",
                "The 2021 Amendment abolished the captive vs merchant mine distinction, allowing sale of up to 50% in open market.",
                "The 2023 Amendment delisted 6 atomic minerals (including Lithium and Titanium) for private commercial mining.",
                "The 2023 Amendment created the Exploration Licence (EL) and empowered the Centre to auction 24 critical minerals."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "The legislative trajectory from MMDR 2015 (auctions & DMF) to MMDR 2023 (critical minerals, private exploration licenses, lithium commercialization) marks India's transition toward energy transition minerals and global supply chain security."
            ],
