            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Critical & Strategic Minerals of India: Supply Security & High-Tech Industry</h3>\n            <p>The global transition toward green renewable energy, electric mobility, semiconductor fabrication, aerospace, and advanced defense technologies has triggered an intense international race for <strong>critical and strategic minerals</strong>. In June 2023, the Ministry of Mines released the historic report <em>'Critical Minerals for India'</em>, identifying <strong>24 critical minerals</strong> vital for economic sovereignty.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Defining a 'Critical Mineral'</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>A mineral is designated as <strong>critical</strong> when it satisfies two dual vulnerability parameters:\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>High Economic Vulnerability:</strong> Indispensable for core manufacturing, green energy technologies (solar PV cells, wind turbines, EV batteries), electronic semiconductors, computing, and defense systems with virtually zero viable substitutes.</li>\n                        <li><strong>High Supply Risk:</strong> Sourced through geographically concentrated supply chains, geopolitical choke-points, or where domestic production is negligible, creating vulnerability to foreign trade embargoes or cartelization.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The 24 Identified Critical Minerals of India</h4>\n            <p>The Ministry of Mines expert committee identified 24 critical and strategic minerals for India:</p>\n            <table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; width: 100%;\">\n                <tr style=\"background-color: #f0f0f0;\">\n                    <th>Mineral Category</th>\n                    <th>Minerals Included</th>\n                    <th>Primary Strategic / High-Tech Applications</th>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Battery & EV Minerals</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Lithium, Cobalt, Nickel, Graphite</strong></td>\n                    <td>Lithium-ion battery cathodes and anodes, electric vehicles, grid-scale battery energy storage systems (BESS).</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Semiconductors & Electronics</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Gallium, Germanium, Indium, Silicon, Tellurium</strong></td>\n                    <td>Microchip wafers, fiber optics, solar photovoltaic cells, night vision systems, 5G telecommunications.</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Aerospace & Superalloys</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Titanium, Tantalum, Niobium, Beryllium, Tungsten, Hafnium, Molybdenum</strong></td>\n                    <td>Jet engine turbine blades, rocketry, missile guidance systems, surgical implants, heat shields.</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Rare Earth & Specialty Minerals</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Rare Earth Elements (REEs - 17 elements), Platinum Group Elements (PGE - 6 metals)</strong></td>\n                    <td>Permanent magnets for wind turbines and EV traction motors, catalytic converters, lasers, defense radar.</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>Fertilizer & Heavy Industrial</strong></td>\n                    <td><strong>Potash, Phosphate, Bismuth, Antimony, Tin, Vanadium</strong></td>\n                    <td>Soil fertility, fire retardants, soldering alloys, high-strength low-alloy (HSLA) construction steel.</td>\n                </tr>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>3. India’s Critical Import Dependency Landscape</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>100% Import Dependent:</strong> India is completely dependent on foreign imports for <strong>Lithium, Cobalt, Nickel, Niobium, Tantalum, Beryllium, and Germanium</strong>.\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><em>Lithium:</em> Sourced primarily from Australia, Chile, and China.</li>\n                        <li><em>Cobalt:</em> Over 70% of global output originates in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) and is refined in China.</li>\n                        <li><em>Nickel:</em> Sourced from Indonesia, the Philippines, and Russia.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>93% to 95% Import Dependent:</strong> For <strong>Copper</strong> concentrates and <strong>Rock Phosphate / Potash</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. Strategic Institutional Initiatives</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The 2023 MMDR Reform:</strong> Delisted 6 atomic minerals (including Lithium, Titanium, Niobium, Tantalum) and authorized the Central Government to auction 24 critical minerals.</li>\n                <li><strong>Tranche Auctions (Nov 2023 onwards):</strong> The Central Government launched the competitive auction of over 38 critical mineral blocks across Jammu & Kashmir, Chhattisgarh, Odisha, Tamil Nadu, and Bihar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mineral Security Partnership (MSP):</strong> In June 2023, India formally joined the US-led 14-nation <strong>Mineral Security Partnership (MSP)</strong> to secure global critical mineral supply chains outside Chinese dominance.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "In June 2023, the Ministry of Mines officially notified a list of 24 Critical Minerals for India.",
                "Criticality is measured by economic vulnerability for green technologies and high geopolitical supply risk.",
                "Key battery minerals include Lithium, Cobalt, Nickel, and Graphite.",
                "India is 100% import-dependent for Lithium, Cobalt, Nickel, Niobium, Tantalum, and REEs.",
                "The Central Government was authorized by MMDR Amendment 2023 to auction 24 critical mineral blocks.",
                "India joined the 14-nation US-led Mineral Security Partnership (MSP) in 2023 to secure global supply chains."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "India's green energy transition, semiconductor mission, and defense preparedness depend on securing the 24 critical minerals through domestic auctions and international partnerships like the MSP."
            ],
