            "title": "Mineral Concession & Auction System: e-Auctions, Composite Licences & Revenue Sharing",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Mineral Concession & Auction System: Transparency & Revenue Architecture</h3>\n            <p>Prior to 2015, mineral concessions in India were allocated through the discretionary, opaque 'First-Come-First-Served' method, causing severe litigation, corruption scandals, and regulatory stagnation. The MMDR Amendment Act, 2015 and subsequent reforms established an objective, market-driven, and transparent concession regime.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Hierarchy of Mineral Concessions</h4>\n            <p>Under the amended MMDR Act, mineral concessions are granted under five distinct legal instruments:</p>\n            <ol>\n                <li><strong>Reconnaissance Permit (RP) / Non-Exclusive Reconnaissance Permit (NERP):</strong> Issued for preliminary regional surveys, airborne geophysics, and geochemical sampling across large tracts without exclusivity.</li>\n                <li><strong>Prospecting Licence (PL):</strong> Granted to explore and locate mineral deposits, evaluate ore grade, and determine commercial feasibility through core drilling and trenching.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mining Lease (ML):</strong> The statutory right to undertake commercial extraction, quarrying, and dispatch of minerals from a demarcated block for a fixed tenure of <strong>50 years</strong>. Granted when a mineral deposit has established <em>G2 (General Exploration) or G1 (Detailed Exploration)</em> feasibility.</li>\n                <li><strong>Composite Licence (Prospecting Licence-cum-Mining Lease):</strong> Introduced in 2015 for blocks with preliminary G3 (Prospecting) data. The bidder undertakes detailed exploration (PL stage) and automatically transitions to commercial extraction (ML stage) upon proving feasibility.</li>\n                <li><strong>Exploration Licence (EL):</strong> Introduced in 2023 specifically for 29 deep-seated and critical minerals, authorizing private exploration entities to explore across up to 1,000 sq km for 5 years.</li>\n            </ol>\n\n            <h4>2. The Electronic Auction Mechanism (e-Auction)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Bidding Platform:</strong> Conducted transparently via national digital portals (e.g., MSTC e-commerce platform).</li>\n                <li><strong>The Bidding Parameter:</strong> Auctions do not use one-off lump-sum cash bids. Instead, bidders compete on the <strong>Percentage of the Value of Mineral Dispatched</strong> (known as the <em>Auction Premium</em>):\n                    <br><em>Total Payment to State = Statutory Royalty + Statutory DMF Levy + Statutory NMET Levy + Auction Premium Bid (%)</em>.\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Two-Stage Ascending Auction:</strong> Bidders submit technical bids and initial price offers; the top technical bidders advance to live electronic reverse/forward bidding rounds, ensuring maximum revenue discovery for the exchequer.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Revenue-Sharing vs Profit-Sharing</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>India’s mining regime operates strictly on a <strong>production and dispatch revenue-sharing model</strong>, not profit-sharing.</li>\n                <li>This ensures that mining companies cannot evade state payments through aggressive accounting write-offs or profit-shifting, guaranteeing steady revenues to State Governments as long as mineral is dispatched from the pit-head.</li>\n                <li>Auction premiums frequently exceed 100% of the statutory royalty in high-grade iron ore blocks (e.g., in Odisha and Karnataka auctions), creating enormous fiscal windfalls for mineral-rich states.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Competitive electronic auctions (e-auctions) became the sole statutory mode for granting major mineral leases in 2015.",
                "Mining Lease (ML) is granted for a uniform tenure of 50 years on proved deposits (G1/G2 exploration).",
                "Composite Licence (PL-cum-ML) allows an entity to prospect (G3) and automatically transition to mining.",
                "Exploration Licence (EL) was introduced in 2023 for 29 deep-seated/critical minerals over up to 1,000 sq km.",
                "The bidding parameter in e-auctions is the 'Percentage of Value of Mineral Dispatched' (Auction Premium).",
                "The revenue-sharing model ensures state governments receive royalties and premiums based on dispatched value."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "The transition from discretionary first-come allocation to digital competitive e-auctions based on the percentage of dispatched mineral value established transparent price discovery and eliminated resource monopolies."
            ],
