            "id": "m10",
            "title": "GSI, IBM & The United Nations Framework Classification (UNFC) System",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>GSI, IBM & The UNFC Mineral Classification System</h3>\n            <p>Geological mapping, mineral inventorying, statutory conservation, and international reporting in India are anchored by two premier scientific and regulatory institutions under the Ministry of Mines: the <strong>Geological Survey of India (GSI)</strong> and the <strong>Indian Bureau of Mines (IBM)</strong>, utilizing the globally standardized <strong>United Nations Framework Classification (UNFC)</strong> system.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Geological Survey of India (GSI)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Origins & Headquarters:</strong> Founded in <strong>1851</strong> (primarily to locate coal deposits for the East India Company's railways). Headquartered in <strong>Kolkata</strong>, it is one of the oldest geological organizations in the world.</li>\n                <li><strong>Core Functions:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Baseline geological mapping of India’s onshore and offshore territory.</li>\n                        <li>Executing regional geochemical (NGCM) and geophysical (NGPM) mapping programs.</li>\n                        <li>Carrying out greenfield mineral prospecting and publishing official geological maps.</li>\n                        <li>Providing geological advisory services for major civil engineering projects (dams, tunnels, landslides).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Indian Bureau of Mines (IBM)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Origins & Headquarters:</strong> Established in <strong>1948</strong> based on the recommendations of the National Mineral Policy Conference. Headquartered in <strong>Nagpur</strong> (Maharashtra).</li>\n                <li><strong>Core Functions:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Regulates systematic, scientific mining and mineral conservation under the <strong>Mineral Conservation and Development Rules (MCDR)</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Approves mandatory <strong>Mining Plans</strong>, Scheme of Mining, and Final Mine Closure Plans (FMCP) before any mine can extract ore.</li>\n                        <li>Maintains the National Mineral Information Repository and compiles the comprehensive <strong>Indian Minerals Yearbook (IMYB)</strong>.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The United Nations Framework Classification (UNFC-1997) System</h4>\n            <p>India officially adopted the <strong>UNFC-1997</strong> system to align its national mineral inventories with global reporting standards. UNFC uses a numerical <strong>three-digit code (E-F-G)</strong> based on three independent axes:</p>\n            <table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; width: 100%;\">\n                <tr style=\"background-color: #f0f0f0;\">\n                    <th>Axis</th>\n                    <th>Sub-Axes Stages</th>\n                    <th>Significance in Mineral Resource Estimation</th>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>G - Geological Axis</strong> (3rd Digit)</td>\n                    <td>\n                        <strong>G1:</strong> Detailed Exploration<br>\n                        <strong>G2:</strong> General Exploration<br>\n                        <strong>G3:</strong> Prospecting<br>\n                        <strong>G4:</strong> Reconnaissance\n                    </td>\n                    <td>Quantifies geological confidence. G1 represents highest confidence with close-interval drilling; G4 represents preliminary satellite/regional airborne indicators.</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>F - Feasibility Axis</strong> (2nd Digit)</td>\n                    <td>\n                        <strong>F1:</strong> Feasibility Study / Mining Report<br>\n                        <strong>F2:</strong> Pre-Feasibility Study<br>\n                        <strong>F3:</strong> Geological Study\n                    </td>\n                    <td>Evaluates technical, engineering, metallurgical, legal, and environmental viability of extracting the mineral deposit.</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                    <td><strong>E - Economic Axis</strong> (1st Digit)</td>\n                    <td>\n                        <strong>E1:</strong> Economically Viable<br>\n                        <strong>E2:</strong> Potentially Economic<br>\n                        <strong>E3:</strong> Intrinsically Economic\n                    </td>\n                    <td>Assesses commercial profitability under prevailing market prices, royalty rates, and production costs.</td>\n                </tr>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>4. Reserves vs Resources under UNFC</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Reserves (Proved / Probable):</strong> Code <strong>111 (Proved Mineral Reserve)</strong> represents an economically viable, technically feasible, and geologically detailed deposit (E1-F1-G1). Code <strong>121 or 122</strong> represents Probable Mineral Reserves.</li>\n                <li><strong>Remaining Resources:</strong> Quantities evaluated at lower exploration stages (G3, G4) or where extraction is currently not commercially viable (E2, E3). <em>Total Mineral Inventory = Reserves + Remaining Resources</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Geological Survey of India (GSI), founded in 1851 at Kolkata, handles baseline geological mapping and exploration.",
                "The Indian Bureau of Mines (IBM), founded in 1948 at Nagpur, approves Mining Plans and enforces mineral conservation (MCDR).",
                "IBM compiles and publishes the comprehensive Indian Minerals Yearbook (IMYB).",
                "India adopted the UNFC-1997 system utilizing a 3-digit code based on Economic (E), Feasibility (F), and Geological (G) axes.",
                "Geological axis ranks confidence from G1 (Detailed Exploration) down to G4 (Reconnaissance).",
                "Code 111 denotes a Proved Mineral Reserve (Economically, Feasibly, and Geologically proved)."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "GSI maps and discovers mineral deposits, IBM regulates their scientific conservation and closure plans, and the UNFC system provides a 3D numerical matrix distinguishing commercially extractable Reserves from theoretical Resources."
            ],
