            "id": "m11",
            "title": "Deep Sea Mining & Offshore Minerals: OAMDR Act, Polymetallic Nodules & Samudrayaan",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Deep Sea Mining & Offshore Minerals: The Ocean Frontiers</h3>\n            <p>With terrestrial mineral reserves depleting and demand for clean-energy metals surging, India has expanded its exploration frontier into the vast ocean floors. Marine minerals contained within India’s Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) and international waters represent the next generation of strategic mineral assets.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Legal Framework: The OAMDR Act & 2023 Reforms</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Offshore Areas Mineral (Development and Regulation) Act, 2002 (OAMDR Act):</strong> Governs the grant of mineral concessions across India's territorial waters (up to 12 nautical miles), contiguous zone (24 nautical miles), and the <strong>Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ - 200 nautical miles)</strong> spanning over 2 million square kilometers.</li>\n                <li><strong>The OAMDR Amendment Act, 2023:</strong> Replaced the earlier discretionary allocation system with <strong>mandatory competitive electronic auctions</strong> for offshore mineral blocks. Permitted private and public corporations to participate in transparent bidding for seabed mineral blocks, operating under strict environmental impact parameters.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Deep-Sea Mineral Types & The Central Indian Ocean Basin (CIOB)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Polymetallic Nodules (PMN):</strong> Potato-shaped mineral concretions resting on the abyssal plains (4,000 to 6,000 meters depth). They contain rich concentrations of <strong>manganese, nickel, copper, and cobalt</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>International Seabed Authority (ISA) Allotment:</strong> India was the first country to be granted the status of a 'Pioneer Investor' in 1987 under UNCLOS. The ISA has allocated India an exploration area of <strong>75,000 square kilometers in the Central Indian Ocean Basin (CIOB)</strong>. Estimated resource potential: approximately 380 million tonnes of nodules containing 4.7 million tonnes of nickel, 4.3 million tonnes of copper, and 0.55 million tonnes of cobalt.</li>\n                <li><strong>Polymetallic Hydrothermal Sulfides (PMS):</strong> Hydrothermal vent deposits along the Central and South-West Indian Ridges rich in copper, zinc, gold, and silver (India holds a 10,000 sq km ISA exploration block).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Deep Ocean Mission & The Samudrayaan Project</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Approved in 2021 by the Ministry of Earth Sciences with a budget outlay of over ₹4,000 crore to develop deep-sea exploration technologies.</li>\n                <li><strong>Samudrayaan & Matsya 6000:</strong> India’s flagship manned deep-submersible vehicle, <strong>Matsya 6000</strong>, engineered by the National Institute of Ocean Technology (NIOT), Chennai. Designed to safely carry three human scientists to an extreme ocean depth of <strong>6,000 meters</strong> equipped with robotic arms, sensors, and imaging systems to scout deep-sea mineral fields.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The OAMDR Amendment Act 2023 introduced competitive e-auctions for offshore seabed mineral blocks in India's EEZ.",
                "Polymetallic Nodules (PMN) on abyssal ocean plains are rich in manganese, nickel, copper, and cobalt.",
                "India holds an ISA exploration contract for 75,000 sq km in the Central Indian Ocean Basin (CIOB).",
                "India also explores 10,000 sq km of Polymetallic Hydrothermal Sulfides (PMS) along the Central Indian Ridge.",
                "The Deep Ocean Mission encompasses the Samudrayaan project, deploying the indigenous manned submersible Matsya 6000 to 6,000 m depth."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Deep-sea mining under UNCLOS regulations and the OAMDR Act positions India to bridge its domestic deficits in nickel, cobalt, and copper through seabed polymetallic nodule harvesting."
            ],
