            "id": "m13",
            "title": "Environmental Governance in Mining: EIA Notification 2006, CAMPA & Mine Closure Frameworks",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Environmental Governance in Mining: Statutory Safeguards & Sustainable Frameworks</h3>\n            <p>While mineral extraction is vital for industrial growth, unscientific open-cast and underground mining can cause severe environmental damage: deforestation, topsoil erosion, acid mine drainage (AMD), air pollution from mineral dust, and social displacement. India has erected a multi-layered statutory architecture to ensure environmental sustainability in mining.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Environmental Clearance (EC) under EIA Notification, 2006</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Issued under the <strong>Environment (Protection) Act, 1986</strong>. Every mining proposal requires prior Environmental Clearance:\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Category 'A' Projects:</strong> Mining leases with an area <strong>greater than 100 hectares</strong> (or all coal/uranium mines). Appraised directly at the Central level by the <em>Expert Appraisal Committee (EAC)</em> under the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change (MoEFCC).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Category 'B' Projects:</strong> Mining leases between 5 and 100 hectares. Appraised at the state level by the <em>State Expert Appraisal Committee (SEAC)</em> and approved by the <em>State Environment Impact Assessment Authority (SEIAA)</em>. (Sub-divided into B1 requiring full EIA study and public hearing, and B2 exempted from formal public hearing).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Public Hearing (Jan Sunwai):</strong> Mandatory transparent public consultation conducted by the State Pollution Control Board (SPCB) near the mine site, recording local villagers' environmental and resettlement concerns.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Forest Clearance & Compensatory Afforestation (CAMPA)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980:</strong> Diversion of any reserved or protected forest land for non-forest mining activity requires prior Central Forest Clearance (Stage-I and Stage-II approval).</li>\n                <li><strong>The Compensatory Afforestation Fund Act, 2016 (CAMPA):</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Mining companies must pay the <strong>Net Present Value (NPV)</strong> of the diverted forest land and fund compensatory afforestation on equivalent non-forest land or twice the area of degraded forest land.</li>\n                        <li>Funds are shared between the <strong>National Compensatory Afforestation Fund (10%)</strong> and the respective <strong>State Compensatory Afforestation Funds (90%)</strong> for afforestation, wildlife habitat protection, and soil conservation.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Mine Closure & Progressive Ecosystem Rehabilitation</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Under the <strong>Mineral Conservation and Development Rules (MCDR)</strong> enforced by the Indian Bureau of Mines:\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Progressive Mine Closure Plan (PMCP):</strong> Ongoing ecological restoration carried out concurrently with mineral extraction over the 50-year lease (backfilling of voids, topsoil conservation, green belt barriers).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Final Mine Closure Plan (FMCP):</strong> Executed at least five years before scheduled exhaustion of the deposit, covering water body conversions, toxic slope stabilization, afforestation, and financial escrow sureties (bank guarantees deposited with IBM).</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Prior Environmental Clearance (EC) is mandatory for all mining under the EIA Notification 2006.",
                "Category A mining projects (>100 hectares, coal, uranium) are appraised centrally by MoEFCC.",
                "Category B projects (5–100 hectares) are appraised at the state level by SEIAA/SEAC.",
                "Compensatory Afforestation Fund (CAMPA) Act 2016 collects NPV and afforestation levies (90% to States, 10% to Centre).",
                "MCDR mandates both a Progressive Mine Closure Plan (PMCP) and a Final Mine Closure Plan (FMCP) with financial bank guarantees."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "India's environmental mining regime balances mineral extraction against conservation through EIA clearances, CAMPA compensatory afforestation funds, and IBM-regulated progressive mine closure plans."
            ],
