            "title": "Mineral Beneficiation & Value Addition: Ore Dressing, Coal Washing & Pelletization",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Mineral Beneficiation & Value Addition: Upgrading National Resources</h3>\n            <p><strong>Mineral beneficiation</strong> (or ore dressing) encompasses the physical, mechanical, and physico-chemical processes applied to raw run-of-mine (ROM) ore to separate gangue minerals, increase the concentration of the target metal (tenor), and convert unmarketable low-grade ore into industrial-grade feedstock.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Primary Beneficiation Techniques</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Comminution & Sizing:</strong> Mechanical crushing (jaw and cone crushers) and grinding (ball mills) to liberate valuable mineral grains from the enclosing host rock matrix, followed by vibrating screen classification.</li>\n                <li><strong>Gravity Separation:</strong> Exploit differences in specific gravity between heavy ore minerals and lighter silica gangue. Utilizes spiral concentrators, shaking tables, and jigs (widely used in iron ore, chromite, and beach sand processing).</li>\n                <li><strong>Froth Flotation:</strong> Employs surface chemistry reagents (collectors, frothers, and modifiers) where air bubbles attach to hydrophobic mineral particles, carrying them to the surface as froth. The dominant method for separating <strong>copper, lead, and zinc sulfide ores</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Magnetic Separation:</strong> High-intensity and low-intensity magnetic separators to extract ferromagnetic magnetite and paramagnetic hematite/manganese from non-magnetic silica and alumina gangue.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Coal Washing: Mitigating High-Ash Indian Coal</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Ash Challenge:</strong> Indian Gondwana coal is drift-origin coal, intimately intergrown with mineral matter, resulting in a naturally <strong>high ash content (35% to 45%)</strong> and low calorific value.</li>\n                <li><strong>Beneficiation Process:</strong> Coal washeries employ dense medium cyclones and heavy media baths to separate heavier rock impurities from lighter pure carbon coal, reducing ash content to 18%–20% for metallurgical coking coal in blast furnaces and 30% for thermal power generation.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Iron Ore Pelletization & Sintering</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Historically, manual mining dumped millions of tonnes of <strong>low-grade iron ore fines and slimes (<60% Fe)</strong> as waste, causing environmental silting of rivers and loss of resources.</li>\n                <li><strong>Pelletization:</strong> Iron ore ultrafines (<100 mesh) are ground with bentonite binder and rolled into uniform 9–16 mm spherical green balls, then heat-hardened in rotary induration kilns at 1,200°C–1,300°C into high-grade pellets (64% to 66% Fe).</li>\n                <li>Pellets possess uniform size, high cold-crushing strength, and excellent reducibility, serving as optimal feedstock for <strong>Direct Reduced Iron (DRI / Sponge Iron)</strong> and blast furnaces.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Mineral beneficiation processes raw run-of-mine (ROM) ore to increase metal tenor and eliminate gangue.",
                "Froth flotation is the principal separation technique for copper, lead, and zinc sulfide ores.",
                "Indian Gondwana coal has a high intrinsic ash content (35%–45%), requiring dense-media washing.",
                "Pelletization transforms discarded low-grade iron ore fines into high-grade spherical pellets (64%+ Fe).",
                "Pellets and sinters enhance blast furnace efficiency and minimize slag formation and carbon emissions."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Value addition through domestic coal washing, froth flotation, and iron ore pelletization prevents the export of raw low-grade fines and maximizes resource conservation."
            ],
