            "id": "m17",
            "title": "Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) & Ease of Doing Business in the Mining Sector",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) & Ease of Doing Business in Mining</h3>\n            <p>Historically characterized by state monopoly and bureaucratic bottlenecks, India’s mining sector has undergone systemic liberalization. Progressive FDI policy revisions, digital single-window clearances, and the decriminalization of minor offenses have integrated Indian mining with global capital markets.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) Norms in Mining</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Non-Fuel Minerals (Metallic & Industrial):</strong> <strong>100% FDI is permitted under the Automatic Route</strong> for exploration, mining, and beneficiation of all non-fuel and non-atomic minerals. Foreign investors require no prior approval from the Reserve Bank of India or the Ministry of Mines.</li>\n                <li><strong>Coal & Lignite:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>In 2019, the Union Cabinet approved <strong>100% FDI under the Automatic Route for commercial coal mining</strong>, coal processing plants (washeries), and associated logistics infrastructure.</li>\n                        <li>Ended the 46-year-old state monopoly of Coal India Limited (CIL) dating back to the Coal Mines (Nationalisation) Act of 1973, allowing international mining giants to participate in commercial coal block auctions.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Titanium & Beach Sand Minerals:</strong> Subject to specific government approval guidelines and Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) oversight to safeguard strategic atomic materials.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Technological & Regulatory Ease of Doing Business Reforms</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>National Single Window System (NSWS):</strong> A unified digital clearance portal integrating mining lease approvals, forest clearances (PARIVESH portal), wildlife board sanctions, and pollution control consent (CTE/CTO) into a single digital application.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mining Tenement System (MTS):</strong> A comprehensive, cloud-based software system developed by the Indian Bureau of Mines that automates the entire concession lifecycle: spatial GIS block demarcation, lease execution, production returns, inspection reports, and royalty reconciliation.</li>\n                <li><strong>Decriminalization of Minor Offenses:</strong> The MMDR Amendment Act, 2021 decriminalized numerous minor administrative and procedural violations (e.g., minor delays in submitting statutory reports), substituting criminal prosecutions with civil compoundable monetary penalties. Serious offenses involving illegal extraction, theft, and boundary breaches remain strictly criminalized under Section 21.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "100% FDI is permitted under the Automatic Route for exploration, mining, and processing of non-fuel minerals.",
                "100% FDI under Automatic Route was approved in 2019 for commercial coal mining and washeries.",
                "PARIVESH and the National Single Window System (NSWS) streamline environmental and forest approvals.",
                "The Mining Tenement System (MTS) by IBM digitally automates mineral concession administration.",
                "The 2021 MMDR Amendment decriminalized minor administrative infractions to improve investor confidence."
            ],
